Dis 1 & 2 — JDBC (DELETE & UPDATE)
Java uses JDBC to connect to a MySQL database. 
executeUpdate() runs SQL commands like DELETE and UPDATE, and returns how many rows were affected. 
You need a driver, connection URL, and Statement object to execute queries.

Dis 3 — Servlet
A Servlet is a Java class that runs on a web server and handles HTTP requests.
 It uses doGet() to receive requests and sends back HTML responses. 
The URL has two parts: the Context-Path (app name) and URL-Pattern (servlet path).

Dis 4 — JSP
JSP lets you write Java code inside HTML pages using <%= %> tags. 
It's simpler than Servlets for displaying dynamic content like dates or user data. 
The server processes the Java, then sends plain HTML to the browser.

Dis 5 — Java Networking

Java networking allows two programs to communicate over a network using sockets.

Server uses ServerSocket to listen on a port and waits for connections using accept()
Client uses Socket to connect to the server's IP and port
Once connected, both sides use InputStream and OutputStream to send and receive data
This follows the Client-Server model where the client makes requests and the server responds
Communication is done over TCP/IP which ensures reliable data transfer

Dis 6 — Hibernate
Hibernate is an ORM framework that lets you work with database records as Java objects — no raw SQL needed. 
You annotate a class with @Entity, use Session to save or retrieve data, and Hibernate handles the SQL automatically.


Hibernate is a Java framework that maps Java objects to database tables — called ORM (Object-Relational Mapping).

Instead of writing raw SQL, you work with Java classes and objects
A class annotated with @Entity represents a database table
@Id marks the primary key field
SessionFactory creates database sessions
session.save() inserts a record, session.get() retrieves one
Hibernate handles the SQL behind the scenes automatically
Configuration is done through hibernate.cfg.xml (database URL, driver, dialect)
